WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.297 --> 00:00:02.430
- For almost a century,
it was accepted that

00:00:02.430 --> 00:00:05.640
the holes in Swiss cheese
came from carbon dioxide,

00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:07.530
produced by the fermentation bacteria

00:00:07.530 --> 00:00:09.540
that convert milk to cheese,

00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:12.180
and that just sort of...
happened automatically.

00:00:12.180 --> 00:00:13.890
That's written in countless articles.

00:00:13.890 --> 00:00:17.040
It's what's all over the internet,
and it's almost true.

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:19.620
About 30 years ago, Swiss cheese
makers started to notice that

00:00:19.620 --> 00:00:21.810
the holes, the technical term is eyes,

00:00:21.810 --> 00:00:23.550
were getting smaller and less frequent,

00:00:23.550 --> 00:00:25.500
and scientists wanted to work out why.

00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:28.320
Specifically, the scientists
here at Agroscope,

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:31.230
a Swiss government-backed
agricultural research facility,

00:00:31.230 --> 00:00:34.230
where, for science, they have
a lot of samples of cheese,

00:00:34.230 --> 00:00:38.280
and, more importantly, samples
of the bacteria that make it.

00:00:38.280 --> 00:00:42.780
- It's skim milk, and about
one to five billion cells of

00:00:42.780 --> 00:00:44.790
lactic acid bacteria per milliliter.

00:00:44.790 --> 00:00:47.550
They have a shelf life
of about 10 to 14 days,

00:00:47.550 --> 00:00:49.410
so all year round, every week,

00:00:49.410 --> 00:00:52.500
the cultures are freshly made
and sent to our customers.

00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:54.540
We are a research station.

00:00:54.540 --> 00:00:56.760
We have our collection of strains,

00:00:56.760 --> 00:00:59.520
but we are also a producer
for these cultures.

00:00:59.520 --> 00:01:02.010
We have the genomes of
almost all the bacteria

00:01:02.010 --> 00:01:03.720
that we have in the collection here,

00:01:03.720 --> 00:01:06.480
and we know what parts of the genome

00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:09.660
are responsible for some
effects on the cheese.

00:01:09.660 --> 00:01:11.820
- From the beginning of the 20th century,

00:01:11.820 --> 00:01:14.490
microbiologists went
to the different dairies

00:01:14.490 --> 00:01:15.600
all over Switzerland

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:18.180
and took the cheese
and different products,

00:01:18.180 --> 00:01:21.210
like whey or the natural whey cultures,

00:01:21.210 --> 00:01:23.820
and isolated bacteria from there.

00:01:23.820 --> 00:01:26.430
At that time, each cheese dairy

00:01:26.430 --> 00:01:30.308
had its own culture to
produce its own cheese,

00:01:30.308 --> 00:01:34.140
and each one of these
cultures was the result of

00:01:34.140 --> 00:01:37.890
decades or centuries of
evolution, of selection.

00:01:37.890 --> 00:01:40.704
Many of these dairies had to close down

00:01:40.704 --> 00:01:44.550
or they switched to the
commercial starter cultures,

00:01:44.550 --> 00:01:47.280
and these bacteria that
made up the cultures

00:01:47.280 --> 00:01:50.370
would have disappeared if
they hadn't been collected.

00:01:50.370 --> 00:01:52.590
So, the collection still expands.

00:01:52.590 --> 00:01:55.770
We have around 15,000 isolates.

00:01:55.770 --> 00:01:57.780
All of the strains of our collection

00:01:57.780 --> 00:02:02.490
are frozen at -80°, and for
the most important ones,

00:02:02.490 --> 00:02:04.920
we also freeze-dry them.

00:02:04.920 --> 00:02:09.060
Each strain that we store here
has a backup on another site.

00:02:09.060 --> 00:02:12.210
- So, each cheese has its
own unique bacterial culture

00:02:12.210 --> 00:02:13.800
stored in the archive back there

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:16.860
and regularly produced
in these bioreactors.

00:02:16.860 --> 00:02:18.780
The difference between
Emmental and Gruyère

00:02:18.780 --> 00:02:20.190
is partly to do with technique,

00:02:20.190 --> 00:02:22.170
but it's also the bacteria that make it,

00:02:22.170 --> 00:02:24.510
and those bacteria are valuable.

00:02:24.510 --> 00:02:27.300
- All the bacteria that we
have here in the collection

00:02:27.300 --> 00:02:29.340
are owned by the Swiss dairy industry

00:02:29.340 --> 00:02:31.800
to have a competitive advantage.

00:02:31.800 --> 00:02:34.290
Our bacteria are not
better than other bacteria,

00:02:34.290 --> 00:02:36.480
but they're very regional, very special.

00:02:36.480 --> 00:02:38.820
- Some of the cheese types
are legally protected,

00:02:38.820 --> 00:02:43.820
and to do that, they have to
use some special bacteria.

00:02:44.100 --> 00:02:46.174
We call them marker bacteria,

00:02:46.174 --> 00:02:50.260
because they bear a very
specific sequence of DNA

00:02:50.260 --> 00:02:52.680
that we are able to detect

00:02:52.680 --> 00:02:54.510
in the cheese during the production,

00:02:54.510 --> 00:02:56.760
but also at the end of the ripening,

00:02:56.760 --> 00:03:00.060
and by detecting this small piece of DNA,

00:03:00.060 --> 00:03:03.810
we are able to say, "Okay,
this is the right cheese."

00:03:03.810 --> 00:03:06.660
- It turns out that
cheese fraud is a thing.

00:03:06.660 --> 00:03:09.870
There's a market for
fake, cheap Swiss cheese,

00:03:09.870 --> 00:03:12.180
and in everyday English,
particularly in North America,

00:03:12.180 --> 00:03:15.480
there's also no distinction
between Swiss-type cheese

00:03:15.480 --> 00:03:17.400
and the specific Swiss cheeses

00:03:17.400 --> 00:03:20.040
that are made here near the
Alps, the ones that have

00:03:20.040 --> 00:03:22.770
legal protected status
marked on the packaging.

00:03:22.770 --> 00:03:25.440
So, Swiss dairies can work
with the researchers here,

00:03:25.440 --> 00:03:28.350
they can order the bacterial
cultures, or just get advice,

00:03:28.350 --> 00:03:31.350
but it is strictly just for
the industry in Switzerland.

00:03:31.350 --> 00:03:34.230
There are trade secrets here
that are guarded carefully,

00:03:34.230 --> 00:03:37.950
and which I was not allowed
to even see, let alone film.

00:03:37.950 --> 00:03:40.530
- We regularly develop new cultures

00:03:40.530 --> 00:03:42.300
together with the cheese industry,

00:03:42.300 --> 00:03:43.920
but because you have always to wait

00:03:43.920 --> 00:03:45.660
the ripening time for the cheeses,

00:03:45.660 --> 00:03:49.050
it takes always several years
to develop a new culture.

00:03:49.050 --> 00:03:50.040
- So, a few years ago,

00:03:50.040 --> 00:03:52.350
the team here had to answer the question,

00:03:52.350 --> 00:03:54.570
why aren't there holes
in the cheese anymore?

00:03:54.570 --> 00:03:55.950
- We produce here the cheese,

00:03:55.950 --> 00:03:58.200
but then they go down to the cellar.

00:03:58.200 --> 00:04:00.600
Most of the cheeses have to age

00:04:00.600 --> 00:04:03.150
between 3 months and 12 months.

00:04:03.150 --> 00:04:05.760
At several time points, we take samples

00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:07.680
to analyze them in the laboratory.

00:04:07.680 --> 00:04:09.720
For the analysis of the holes,

00:04:09.720 --> 00:04:14.700
we use X-ray and we use
also computer tomography,

00:04:14.700 --> 00:04:17.790
so then we really can count the holes,

00:04:17.790 --> 00:04:20.340
but we also see the
distribution in the cheese.

00:04:20.340 --> 00:04:23.910
- The bacteria that are
responsible for making these holes

00:04:23.910 --> 00:04:28.320
produce propionate,
acetate, and carbon dioxide,

00:04:28.320 --> 00:04:31.170
and this carbon dioxide
is produced in the cheese

00:04:31.170 --> 00:04:35.460
and aggregates all around impurities.

00:04:35.460 --> 00:04:39.270
The more the bacterium
produces this carbon dioxide,

00:04:39.270 --> 00:04:41.700
it accumulates and builds these holes.

00:04:41.700 --> 00:04:44.760
These impurities capture
the carbon dioxide

00:04:44.760 --> 00:04:47.640
and then a bubble forms and grows.

00:04:47.640 --> 00:04:49.740
- They found that the milk was too clean,

00:04:49.740 --> 00:04:52.260
so we didn't have any dust in it,

00:04:52.260 --> 00:04:55.200
and this was because we
had closed milking systems

00:04:55.200 --> 00:04:57.870
so the dust could not get into the milk.

00:04:57.870 --> 00:04:59.940
- Everything improved the last decade.

00:04:59.940 --> 00:05:03.870
So, the milking process is
hermetically closed now,

00:05:03.870 --> 00:05:06.480
and in former times, in the barn,

00:05:06.480 --> 00:05:09.000
you had always this hay dust everywhere,

00:05:09.000 --> 00:05:10.620
and it came also into the milk.

00:05:10.620 --> 00:05:14.580
- We tried different
particles to put into the milk

00:05:14.580 --> 00:05:17.820
to see if the holes are growing again.

00:05:17.820 --> 00:05:22.020
Hay powder is the best one,
and we really could see that

00:05:22.020 --> 00:05:24.360
the hole formation was dependent on

00:05:24.360 --> 00:05:28.020
the concentration of the
hay powder we put in it.

00:05:28.020 --> 00:05:30.690
It's only one milligram
per thousand liters.

00:05:30.690 --> 00:05:32.760
- Modern food safety standards mean that

00:05:32.760 --> 00:05:36.030
milk is far cleaner now than
it ever was in the past,

00:05:36.030 --> 00:05:39.330
without the tiny...
well, these days, it's "imperfections".

00:05:39.330 --> 00:05:41.280
In the past, it would
have been contaminants,

00:05:41.280 --> 00:05:44.640
but, without those, if the
milk is perfectly clean,

00:05:44.640 --> 00:05:46.530
the holes just don't form,

00:05:46.530 --> 00:05:48.990
and while those holes don't
really affect the taste,

00:05:48.990 --> 00:05:50.010
they certainly affect

00:05:50.010 --> 00:05:53.073
whether Swiss cheese
looks like Swiss cheese.

